$\mathrm{N}^{6}$ -methyladenosine $(\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A})$ has been identified as one of the most abundant RNA modifications, and it is a dynamic and reversible process of RNA methylation modification that omnipresent in RNA of eukaryotic and nuclear-replicating viruses.1,2 Cumulative studies have provided evidence that variations in the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ levels provide diverse biological functions in mammals, such as cellular heat shock response,3 embryogenesis,4 transcription splicing,5 nuclear export,6 circadian rhythm,7 protein translation control,8 and proliferation/differentiation of stem cells.9 The regulatory mechanism of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ modification may be involved in diseases, especially tumorigenesis.10 Therefore, a simple, rapid, and sensitive method for $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA determination is of biological importance. However, the content of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ in isolated RNA has been estimated to be very low with only approximately $3{-}5~\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ sites per messenger RNA (mRNA), let alone in the total RNA.11,12 The low abundance of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ increases the difficulty of its accurate detection. Moreover, the methylation of adenosine does not impede its pairing with thymidine or uracil, making it hardly detectable with reverse transcription-based methods. Although $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ was first discovered in 1974, due to technical limitations, the detection of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ , especially the quantification of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ , or even the identification of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ from the single base level has been progressing slowly for a long time.
Until now, different techniques for $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA detection have been proposed. In early studies, some traditional techniques, including capillary electrophoresis,13 thin layer chromatography,14 paper chromatography,15 reversed-phase high-performance liquid chromatography,16 and high-performance liquid chromatography17 have been reported for $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ detection. However, these techniques require labeling RNA with radioactive $\mathrm{P}^{32}$ or require costly and large instruments; however, $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ methylation is detected at the whole level with the detection result often being unsatisfactory. In 2012, two studies proposed the approach of immunoprecipitation followed by a high-throughput sequencing technique, which identified the methylation levels of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ in human and mouse for a wide range and with high throughput at the transcriptional level.2,18 Afterwards, some other techniques based on sequencing were also developed in succession. Although these techniques provided a qualitative analysis of the hypermethylated mRNA region, complicated multistep procedures, time-consuming detection or the requirement of well-trained operators considerably limit their applications for $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA detection with rapidness.
Electrogenerated chemiluminescence (ECL) biosensing method, owing the merits of high sensitivity, easy operation, good controllability, and the equipment is simple and low-cost,19 has attracted considerable attention and offers new opportunities for applications in the determination of biomolecules (such as DNA,20,21 microRNA,22,23 DNA methylation,24,25 and proteins26,27). Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are emerging and promising porous crystalline materials with a well-defined crystal structure, adjustable micropores/channels, large surface area, high load capacity for agents, and chemical and thermal stability,28-30 and they have attracted tremendous attention from researchers. These porous organic-inorganic hybrid materials have been exploited for chemical sensing,31 gas storage/separation,32 heterogeneous catalysis,33,34 and transporting/releasing of drugs,35 etc. Currently, the synthesis and applications of functionalized MOFs for the detection of various targets has become another research hot topic in this field.36-39 For instance, Ge et al. fabricated a novel electrochemical biosensor using gold nanoparticle-functionalized Cu-MOFs to immobilize DNA strands for signal reporting, meanwhile, the functionalized Cu-MOFs with excellent catalytic activity also acted as catalyst for the oxidization of glucose, which resulted to highly sensitive detection of miRNA-155.40 Yuan’s group prepared N-(aminobutyl)-N-(ethylisoluminol) (ABEI)-functionalized MOFs which contained abundant luminescent molecule of ABEI can serve as ECL indicator for mucin1 determination on cancer cells, which provided an improved analytical performance.41 Shao et al. recently in situ synthesized $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}C l_{2}}$ -functionalized MOFs thin film on the electrode surface, which encapsulated numerous $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}C l_{2}}$ within the MOFs framework thus can be employed as ECL indicator for the detection of cardiopathy-related protein, which showed excellent ECL behavior.42 Cao’s group constructed a quenching ECL immunosensor using resonance energy transfer between $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles and Ru@UiO-67 for the determination of insulin.43 Positively charged $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ luminophore was packaged into the electronegative UiO-67 MOFs structure which served as ECL donor, meanwhile, $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles acted as ECL acceptor. Because there were suitable overlaps between the emission spectrum of ECL donor and the ultraviolet absorption spectrum of ECL acceptor, resulting in the occurrence of resonance energy transfer between them, which decreased the ECL intensity. Furthermore, the incorporation of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ enhanced both the luminescence lifetime and the quantum yield of the luminophore due to the space limitation effect of MOF micropores.44 Therefore, functionalized MOFs showed great potential in field of biosensing application. To our knowledge, detection of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA based on $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ -functionalized MOFs has not been reported yet.
In this study, methylated microRNA-21 (miRNA21) was chosen as the model target because miRNA21 is a typical identified miRNA sequence which plays an up-regulating role in various physiological activities and has been widely used in related microRNA researches,45-47 and $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ -functionalized MOFs (Ru@MOFs)
served as the ECL indicator and nanoreactor, thus a simple and sensitive “signal-off” ECL biosensing platform was fabricated to quantitatively detect the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA sequence based on the quenching of luminescent MOFs by ferrocene (Fc) and the high specificity between the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody and $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA. In this protocol, luminescent $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ were synthesized with trimesic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ as the ligand and zinc nitrate as the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ source, and luminescent $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ was incorporated into the MOF structure through a one-pot hydrothermal method. The luminophore of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ was encapsulated into the cavities and adsorbed on the surface of the polyhedral MOF structure. The incorporation of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ into the MOF structure can prolong the residence time of the ECL indicator on the electrode surface, which overcomes the drawback that $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ is easily released when used for aqueous phase detection; additionally, the multipore/channel structure of MOFs can supply a reaction location for relevant ECL reactions, which can substantially enhance the ECL efficiency and stability. To expand the application of functionalized MOFs in the aqueous phase, carboxylic carbon nanotubes (CNTs) were used to prepare Ru@MOFs/CNT composites for improving the electric conductivity and dispersity, at the same time, increasing the loading amount of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody due to the abundant carboxyl of the functionalized CNTs. The -COOH at the edge of the Ru@MOFs was activated and conjugated with the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody. The $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ site in the target RNA sequence can be specifically recognized and captured by the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody through an immunoreaction between the antibody and antigen. When a complementary DNA sequence modified with Fc at the end of $_{3},$ (Fc-DNA) was introduced, it could hybridize with the target $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA to form a double helix structure, which brought Fc near the ECL indicator on the surface of the electrode, leading to a decreased ECL signal intensity as a result of the high quenching efficiency of ferrocene on $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ . Thus, $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA was quantitative detected according to the decrease in ECL intensity. This platform was fabricated without considerable modification, which simplified the detection steps compared with conventional ECL detection.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
The reagents, materials and apparatus are displayed in the Supporting Information.
Preparation of the $\mathbf{Ru\mathcal{Q}M O F s}$ and Ru@MOFs/CNTs composites. The synthesis of the $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ was performed through a solvothermal process that was reported previously with minor modifications.48 Briefly, $80~\mathrm{\mg}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ , $200~\mathrm{\mg}$ of $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $20~\mathrm{\mg}$ of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}C l_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ were mixed into $6~\mathrm{\mL}$ of EtOH/DMF $/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solution $(\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{EtOH}):\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{DMF}):\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}){=}1:1:1)$ followed by 15 min of ultrasonic treatment. Then, the mixture was settled into a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and underwent reaction at $105~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $14\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting yellow/ white products were collected by a centrifuge and washed with EtOH/DMF $/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solution three times, and finally, the products were dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. Then, $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of the obtained $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ were added into $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of the CNT aqueous solution $\mathrm{'}c_{\mathrm{NTs}}=1\ \mathrm{mg/mL}$ ) and sonicated for another $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain a well-dispersed Ru@MOFs/CNTs solution. After centrifuged and washed, the Ru@MOFs/CNT powder was collected. Then, the obtained Ru@MOFs/CNTs were redispersed into $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of a $0.1\%$ chitosan solution to immobilize the composites on the surface of the electrode in a more stable manner.
Fabrication of the ECL Biosensing Platform (Ru@MOFs/CNTs/Ab). First, the surface of a glass carbon electrode (GCE) was cleaned by polishing with 0.3 and 0.05 $\upmu\mathrm{m}\ \mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ successively. After it was dried, $9~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the Ru@MOFs/CNT dispersion was covered on the surface of the GCE. Subsequently, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of EDC/NHS ( $\mathrm{100~mM}$ :
$25~\mathrm{mM},$ solution was used to activate the carboxyl group in the $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . After removing redundant reactants, the dried modified electrode was immersed in 10 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody $(10~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Then, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $0.25\%$ bovine serum albumin (BSA) was added to the surface of the modified GCE at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to block the remaining active sites for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Followed by rinsing with $10\mathrm{mM}$ PBS buffer, $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of TE buffer containing different amounts of target methylated RNA were dripped onto the electrode surface for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Then, the surface of the electrode was rinsed thoroughly with PBS to remove the unreacted $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA. Finally, the modified GCE was incubated with $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ PBS that contained $1~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ of Fc-DNA for hybridization with target RNA for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . Subsequently, the modified electrode was rinsed with $10~\mathrm{mM}$ PBS buffer to eliminate redundant Fc-DNA. Then, the obtained modified electrode served as the working electrode.
ECL Determination (Ru@MOFs/CNTs/Ab/m6A/Fc-DNA). ECL measurements were carried out using a commercial ECL system with a scanning range of $0\sim1.3\mathrm{~V~}$ at a scanning rate of $50\mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , and the voltage was set to $-800\mathrm{V}$ . The modified GCE served as the working electrode, and a platinum wire and an $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ electrode acted as the auxiliary electrode and reference electrode, respectively. The test solution was $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of PBS (0.1 M, pH 7.4) with $50~\mathrm{mM}$ tripropylamine (TPA) as the coreactant. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was performed using a CHI 660D electrochemical station in PBS (10 mM, pH 7.4) that contained $5\ \mathrm{mM\Fe(CN)}_{6}{}^{3-}/4^{-}$ and 0.1 M KCl with a frequency range of $10^{-1}$ to $10^{5}\mathrm{Hz}$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Principle of $\mathbf{m^{6}A}$ RNA Detection. As illustrated in Scheme 1, first, Ru@MOFs/CNTs were coated on the surface of GCE, which generated a very strong initial ECL signal when a positive voltage was applied to the electrode. Because the incorporation of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ into the MOF structure can prolong the residence time of the ECL indicator on the electrode surface, which overcomes the drawback when $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ simply adsorbed on the surface of the electrode used for aqueous phase detection, the multipore/channel structure of MOFs can supply a reaction location for ECL reactions, which can substantially enhance the ECL efficiency and stability. Second, due to the abundant -COOH groups in Ru@MOFs/CNTs, the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody can be firmly bonded onto the GCE surface through a reaction between the carboxyl and amino groups. Third, the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody can specifically recognize the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ site and capture target RNA due to the immunoreaction between the antibody and antigen. Finally, when a complementary DNA sequence modified with ferrocene at the end of 3’ (Fc-DNA) was introduced, it could hybridize with target $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA to form a double helix structure, which brought the Fc near the ECL indicator on the surface of the electrode, leading to a decreased ECL signal intensity because of the high quenching efficiency of ferrocene on $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ . Thus, $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA could be quantitatively detected according to the decrease in ECL intensity.
Scheme 1. Schematic diagram of the ECL platform for $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA determination. (A) Preparation of Ru@MOFs and Ru@MOF/CNT composites and (B) Fabrication and detection principle of the ECL biosensing platform.
Characterization of Materials. The morphologies of the Ru@MOFs and Ru@MOF/CNT composites were investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Figures 1A-C display the SEM images of the Ru@MOFs and Ru@MOF/CNT composites on different scales. From the figures, we observed that Ru@MOFs showed a uniform rod-shaped structure and dispersed uniformly on CNTs, which supplied a large “hotbed” for the $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ . Figure 1D shows the Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectrum of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ (in red) and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ (in black) and luminescent MOF (in blue). As shown, there are two significant changes that occurred after the $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ molecule was incorporated into the frameworks. In the FT-IR spectra of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ , the characteristic peaks $(3087-2554~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ) assigned to carboxylic acid and free COO- (1722 and $1403~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ disappeared, which may be due to the formation of luminescent MOFs. The strong absorption bands at $1437{-}1382\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and $1627{-}1460\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ may be attributed to the vibration of carboxylate anions in $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ and the skeletal vibration of bipyridine/benzene in $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ , respectively.49 X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed to confirm the major elemental components of the Ru@MOFs. As expected, the typical XPS spectra of the Zn, O, N, C, and Ru elements were shown; see Figures 1E-H. These characterizations confirmed the successful incorporation of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ into the synthesized MOF structure. Additionally, as presented in Figure S1, the elemental mapping distributions of the single Ru@MOF polygon also confirmed the primary components of $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Ru in the functionalized MOFs. The Ru element was derived from $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ molecules, which indicated that the luminophor was homogeneously distributed in the framework structure. Moreover, after $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ incorporation, the framework integrity of these MOFs was maintained, as evidenced by the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Figure S2), indicating that the incorporation of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}\mathrm{^{2+}}$ did not affect the original morphology. Furthermore, the $\zeta$ potentials of the different materials were measured to understand their charge distribution in solution. As illustrated in Figure S3, after incorporating with $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ molecules, the surface potential of the MOFs changed from $-4.6$ to $-2.0$ due to the charge neutralization between the $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ cations and electronegative MOFs particles, which indicated the electrostatic interaction between $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ and the MOFs. To validate the incorporation and stability of the $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ , the fluorescence of the Ru@MOFs was tested. A broad emission peak $(\sim615\ \mathrm{nm})$ assigned to $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ and the Ru@MOFs was observed at an excitation of $480\ \mathrm{nm}$ ; however, no emission peak appeared for the pristine MOFs (Figure S4), which further confirmed the successful combination of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ and the MOFs. Furthermore, the obtained Ru $@$ MOFs were washed for different times with a water/ethanol (1:1) mixture, and the corresponding fluorescence emissions of the Ru@MOFs after washing several times almost did not change (Figure S5), which confirmed the luminescent stability of the Ru@MOFs and that no $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ leaked out.
Electrochemical Characterization and Feasibility of the ECL Sensing Platform.
To verify the ECL platform can work as anticipated, EIS was conducted to monitor the changes in the modified electrode surfaces during different assembly procedures. The EIS of the electrodes observed upon the stepwise modification procedures are displayed in Figure 2A. The EIS of the bare GCE displayed an almost straight line with a small semicircle domain (curve a), indicating a low electron transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{et})}$ value of the redox couple of $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{3-/4-}}$ . After GCE was modified with the Ru@MOFs/CNTs, EIS revealed an increased $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ value (curve b), which showed that the Ru@MOFs/CNTs possess poor electroconductivity and that electron transfer was hampered. When the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody was further modified on the electrode, it showed a larger $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ (curve c) compared with the Ru@MOFs/CNTs/GCE because the modified $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody increased the impedance. After BSA was located on the modified electrode, the $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ further increased (curve d). An obvious increase in the $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ value was obtained after the capture of the target $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA (curve e), which was due to the electrostatic repulsion between the phosphate backbone of the oligonucleotides and the negatively charged redox couple probe. After being immersed in a solution of Fc-DNA, the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA on the electrode could capture Fc-DNA through hybridization; thus, the $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ value increased further. The EIS results confirmed that the ECL platform was fabricated successfully.
As the ECL responses show in Figure 2B, the Ru@MOFs/CNTs/GCE showed an extremely strong ECL signal (curve a) at $1.18{\mathrm{V}}$ , which was due to the efficient ECL property of the Ru $@$ MOFs. Then, it slightly decreased after the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody was bonded to the modified GCE (curve b). When BSA and $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA were successively attached to the modified electrode, the ECL intensity further decreased (curve c). This is because the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody and BSA are proteins and RNA have poor electroconductivity, which impedes the electron transfer in ECL reactions. The ECL intensity reached a minimum after Fc-DNA attached to the modified GCE (curve d), which was attributed to the strong quenching effect of ferrocene on $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ . These results confirmed that the proposed ECL sensing method is feasible for $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA detection.
