Optimization of experimental conditions. Three main parameters that affect detection performance were optimized, including the dosage of the Ru $@$ MOF/CNT composites, antibody usage, and the reaction time for the antibody and $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA. The dosage of the Ru@MOF/CNT composites had a considerable impact on the ECL performance. As observed in Figure 3A, as the volume of the Ru@MOF/CNT dispersion that was modified on GCE increased, the ECL intensity gradually increased. A high intensity ECL response was obtained when the dosage of the Ru@MOFs/CNTs increased to $9\upmu\mathrm{L}$ . It was unhelpful to further increase the dosage to enhance the ECL intensity because the surface of GCE is not large enough to accommodate more composites and a thick ECL sensing film is not conductive to detection. Thus, we chose $9~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ as the optimal dosage. To obtain the optimum concentration of the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody, different concentrations of the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody were bonded to the Ru@MOFs/CNTs/GCE, followed by capturing $1~\mathrm{{nM}}$ of the target $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA. The ECL response was recorded and is depicted in Figure 3B, and as the concentration of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody increased from 0.01 to $10~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ , the intensity of ECL gradually weakened. Upon a further increase in the concentration to $20~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ , the ECL intensity tended to level off, indicating that the antibody exerted its maximum function. Therefore, $10~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ antibody was used for the following measurements. Figure 3C shows the impact of the reaction time for the antibody and target $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA $(1\mathrm{\nM})$ on the modified electrode. When the reaction time was extended from 10 to $60\ \mathrm{min}$ , the ECL intensity showed a downward trend. Upon further prolonging the reaction time, the ECL intensity slowly decreased. Considering the detection efficiency, 60 min was employed.
Detection Performance. Under the optimal experimental conditions, different concentrations of methylated $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA were detected using this proposed ECL sensing platform. Figure 4A displays the typical ECL response of the probe for $\mathrm{Fc-DNA/m^{6}A}$ , RNA/BSA $\mathrm{^{\primem}A}$ and antibody/Ru@MOFs/CNTs/GCE to various concentrations of target $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA. As shown in Figure 4A, the ECL intensities presented a good linear relationship with the logarithm of the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA concentration $\operatorname{(lg}\ c)$ in the range of 0.001 to $10\mathrm{\nM}$ . The linear regression equation of $I=$ $3225.03-1843.26\log{(c/\mathrm{nM})}\left(\mathrm{R}=0.9\$ ) was obtained for quantitative detection of the target methylated RNA, and the estimated detection limit was $0.3\mathrm{\bf~pM}$ at a signal-to-noise ratio of $3\sigma$ ( $\scriptstyle{\overbar{\sigma}}$ refers the standard deviation for the blank, $n=8\$ ). In addition, a comparison of the $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA detection performances using different sensing methods is displayed in Table S2 (Supporting Information). The results showed that this “signal-off” ECL strategy is promising for quantitative detection of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA.
Reproducibility, Specificity and Stability of the Proposed ECL Method. The roducibility of this ECL strategy was verified by analysis of the same concentration of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA $\left(0.1{\mathrm{~nM}}\right)$ using ten electrodes prepared in parallel under the same conditions. As depicted in Figure 4B, the reproducibility of this ECL sensing platform was acceptable with a relative standard deviation (RSD) of less than $5.0\%$ .
To investigate the specificity of this ECL strategy, $0.1\mathrm{\nM}$ of unmethylated RNA, single-base mismatched $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA, three-base mismatched $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA, methylated miRNA-155, methylated miRNA-101, target $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA, and a mixed sample were used as the contrast objects to evaluate the specificity. From Figure 4C, we observe that this ECL biosensing platform almost had no quenching response for the unmethylated RNA and other methylated DNA; however, it showed a great ECL decrease for the same concentration of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA and $0.1\ \mathrm{nM}\ \mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA in a mixture with interferences, indicating the high specificity of the proposed ECL biosensing method.
Figure 4D shows the ECL responses of the sensing platform under consecutive cyclic potential scanning for 15 cycles in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of PBS that contained $50~\mathrm{mM}$ TPA for the determination of $0.1~\mathrm{{nM}}$ of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA. We observed that the ECL intensity did not exhibit an obvious change, and the RSD of the ECL intensity was $2.7\%$ . After the fabricated electrode was stored at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for three weeks, the ECL response of the sensing platform for the same $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA increased by $9.3\%$ (Figure S6), which may have been caused by the partial inactivation of $\mathrm{m}^{6}\mathrm{A}$ RNA. Therefore, this ECL biosensing platform possesses good stability.
